K03 memslot_modification_stress_test.
Original, plugin on: neither Image finishes mode 1 in 120 s (GCC 188,605
faults, EvoCC 78,448); per fault EvoCC executes 5.61x GCC.
Equal work (-m 3 -b 256M, default 10 memslot changes, 65,687 faults each):
  VM phase 4,698,438,640 / 865,915,212 = 5.43x (kernel 5.81x)
  plugin wall time 81.6 / 34.2 s (2.39x)
The ranking repeats K02's: ktime_get 19.2%, user_mem_abort 6.0%,
rmqueue_bulk 4.4%, __handle_mm_fault 4.1%. user_mem_abort runs exactly the
same 239,699,677 instructions as in K02; __handle_mm_fault,
do_anonymous_page and zap_present_ptes differ by less than 0.02%.
Memslot code (stage2_unmap_*, srcu, gfn_to_memslot, kvm_set_*) totals
29.3 M / 10.0 M, under 1% of the 3.83 G excess. K02 and K03 share one
measured cause: the per-fault populate, allocate and unmap path.

K11f pidfd_test, unchanged, 45-s limit, plugin on. The minimal payload
adds /bin/sleep (tools/k1/perf/sleep.c), which the poll-exec case needs.
Both Images pass 7 cases and time out in the recycled-PID loop (exit 124).
In that loop GCC completes 4,211 fork/exit cycles in 32.8 s and EvoCC 998
in 32.4 s. Per cycle (EvoCC scaled to equal cycles):
  kernel 3,809,962 / 329,169 = 11.6x; 56.8% of EvoCC kernel instructions
  sit in transport runs (cause 1 below), 2.3% for GCC
  copy_pte_range  1,275,372 /  6,193  206x  36.5% of the excess
  zap_pte_range     249,930 /  6,126   41x   7.0%
  ktime_get         235,662 / 24,308   9.7x  6.1%
  zap_present_ptes  193,258 /  8,361   23x   5.3%
  set_pte_range      95,033 /  5,770   16x   2.6%
EvoCC's copy_pte_range is one 1,329,200-byte function with a 2.5 KiB
frame; about 1,700 instructions run per PTE slot, most slots empty.

K10 test_progs. No retained Image has vmlinux BTF or a matching test_progs
build, so the original cannot run. Two measurements stand in:
1. Retained serial logs (.evidence/kselftest-full/20260923T124453Z, hvf-host,
   old pair f2d59e05/8fa43fb4): printk timestamps bracket group 482 at
   238.4-261.5 s for GCC and 295.2-300.8 s for EvoCC (1.13-1.26x). GCC
   finishes after 285.1 s of its 300-s budget: under 5% margin. The gap
   is a distributed slowdown, not one stalled group.
2. tools/k1/perf/bpf-load-proxy.c, same work on both Images (40 loads of a
   300-iteration bounded-loop socket filter with JIT, 400 test runs, 2,000
   hash-map update/lookup/delete; checksum 31920200 on both):
   kernel 726,601,174 / 193,576,304 = 3.75x. Verifier functions run about
   3x (backtrack_insn 2.8x, __mark_chain_precision 3.1x, is_state_visited
   3.2x, do_check 3.0x); htab_map_update_elem 13x and __kvmalloc_node 19x;
   Waits in ktime_get, mutex_spin_on_owner, smp_call_function_many_cond
   and queued_spin_lock_slowpath take 34% of the excess. Transport runs:
   10.3% versus 1.4% of kernel instructions.

K01b sched_ext full program (retained logs, 20260927T223104Z, TCG
cortex-a72, older pair 62c29ae4/13bd4d0b): GCC passes 23/23 in 52-56 s
(printk brackets); EvoCC passes only tests 1-8 before the 120-s limit.
Its next tests (init_enable_count, reload_loop, prog_run) fork tasks and
load BPF, the paths measured in K11f and the K10 proxy. No sched_ext Image
remains.

Machine-code causes, demand 256 MiB (frame = ldr/str based on sp or x29):
  class   EvoCC          GCC          ratio
  frame   987,316,870    97,030,811   10.2
  move    974,111,266    59,279,182   16.4
  branch  460,060,753   193,125,749    2.4
  call     45,617,973    20,624,157    2.2
  pac      87,825,255    21,075,295    4.2
1. Block-argument transport. Runs of six or more frame loads/stores and
   register moves hold 1,395,735,604 EvoCC kernel instructions (29.6%)
   versus 13,804,604 (1.6%): at least 36% of the excess. Every join
   (if/else, switch, asm goto, loop back edge) passes all live values as
   block arguments. Out-of-SSA copies are made before allocation; spilled
   values reload and store through their own slots, so each edge loads,
   moves and stores them again, sometimes into the same slot. In-run
   share: user_mem_abort 86%, rmqueue_bulk 87%, zap_pte_range 87%,
   __handle_mm_fault 82%, zap_present_ptes 76%. Code size follows:
   rmqueue_bulk 52,656 bytes versus GCC's whole __rmqueue_pcplist 3,880.
2. Time- and contention-scaled work, about 23% of the excess: functions
   whose call counts exceed GCC's by over 1.5x for the same faults.
   ktime_get runs about 1,040 times per second on both Images (tick-bound)
   and spends 2,077 (GCC) or 4,975 (EvoCC) instructions per call in
   seqcount retries; spinlock waits and scheduler load balancing follow.
   They grow with elapsed time, so they amplify cause 1.
3. Small helpers stay out of line: 204 calls per fault to 364 helpers that
   GCC inlines (const_folio_flags 41.6 per fault, rcu_preempt_read_enter
   and _exit 14.5, kvm_pte_table 13.1, folio_flags 10.1). The callees alone
   run 211 M instructions (5.4% of the excess); each has a PAC pair and a
   frame record. Constant index code ("mov #0; and; lsl; add") and
   constant shift-count guards stay unfolded.
4. One-word structs (pte_t, pmd_t) live in stack slots: zero-filled for
   -ftrivial-auto-var-init=zero, copied slot to slot for each by-value
   helper, with a canary; boolean results are set, masked and tested again.
5. Return signing on every function: 1,337 PAC instructions per fault
   versus 321; each is a TCG helper call on the IMPDEF PAuth lane.
6. --ub-checks=checked (the default) leaves 25,595 trap sites in EvoCC
   .text (14,398 shift-count, 4,212 division, 1,780 remainder, 1,274
   signed-overflow, 3,289 unreachable, 642 bit-builtin); GCC has none.
   Executed guards in the top 60 functions are about 0.6% of the excess.
Compiler facts: the kernel build passes none of EvoCC's opt-in
--frame-opt, --isel-opt or --regalloc-opt variants (paired saves, leaf
frames, Boolean controls, copy hints, reload reuse); all are off by default.
CodegenOptions::protects_codegen() would also force them off, because the
kernel uses -fno-omit-frame-pointer, -mgeneral-regs-only and the sysreg
stack guard. With all of them on and that protection lifted, the reductions
shrink by only 10-20% in static instruction lines (asm-goto 982 to 787,
loop 395 to 319), so they do not remove cause 1.
Boot alone (one vCPU) runs 4.61x GCC: keccakf (jitterentropy) 259 M with
its local array in memory, and __free_pages_ok 215 M versus 8 M.

Reductions: tests/fixtures/kernel-codegen-reductions/. Frozen EvoCC
366fd796 (host, --target) versus sealed gcc-14 -O2, both with the kernel's
code-generation flags; each measured object is linked with one GCC-built
driver, booted on the GCC Image (one vCPU) and counted with tbprof.
Checksums match for every pair. Instructions per call:
  reduction                     function         EvoCC     GCC  ratio
  loop-carried-transport.c      free_ok        179,135   8,236   21.7
     (per loop iteration about 175 versus 8)
  asm-goto-live-transport.c     fault              488      67    7.3
  leaf-helper-call.c            folio_state   55+3x10=85     9    9.4
  struct-value-through-stack.c  pte_offset_map     115      23    5.0
  local-array-constant-index.c  theta              329      94    3.5
Reproduce: tools/k1/perf/reduction-counts.py --plugin TBPROF --work NEW
  --source FILE.c:FUNCTION ... (counts.json keeps every number).

QEMU drift. No QEMU 11.0.3 executable (af7f6f87) exists on this machine.
A search of /opt, /usr/local, /Applications, /private/tmp, /private/var/tmp
and the home caches, downloads and dev trees finds only 11.1.2; Homebrew's
cache has only the 11.1.2 bottle; there is no local APFS snapshot; the
private 11.1.1 builds under .build/floating-targeted were retired. No 11.0.3
comparison is possible without an install, and none was made. The existing
records already separate drift from the GCC 7/10 result: on 2026-09-28 one
drifted binary, QEMU 11.1.1 (45d74da5), gave GCC 10/10 on the uniform pair
and 7/10 on the pinned pair. Today, unchanged boot.sh on 11.1.2 with load
47-57 gives GCC demand 1/10 and memslot 1/10, EvoCC 0/10 and 0/10 (all
TIMEOUT 124; baseline-original/runtime.json). The 120-s wall-clock lane is
load-sensitive on both Images; the emulator version is not the measured
variable that moves GCC's count.

